Q1. What is a linked list?

A linear data structure consisting of nodes where each node stores data and a link to another node.

Q2. What is a node?

A node contains data and one or more pointers/links connecting it to other nodes.

Q3. Why is linked-list access O(n)?

Because nodes are not indexed like an array, so we generally have to traverse from the head to reach a particular position.

Q4. What is the head?

A pointer/reference to the first node.

Q5. What does nullptr mean?

It represents a null pointer, meaning it points to no object.

Q6. Why is insertion at the beginning O(1)?

Because we only need to create a node, point it to the current head, and update head.Q9. How do you find the middle?

